# Legacy Omeka S URL repair for the iowmaterialhistorieswebinar.org archive.
#
# This directory is a static export of an Omeka S install, and the export
# appended .html to every page: /s/Material-Histories/item/4 became
# /s/Material-Histories/item/4.html. Everything indexed, cited or bookmarked
# before the migration points at the extensionless form -- including the
# `@id` permalinks Omeka wrote into the JSON-LD block on all 59 resource
# pages, which are what a citation made from this site would have recorded.
#
# Ordering matters. Caddy runs `redir` before `handle`, so these fire ahead of
# the object-storage matcher, and among the `redir`s below file order decides.
# Every specific rule must therefore stay above the generic one at the bottom.
#
# Sibling archives (occupyarchive, virginiaslostat, mallhistory, thanksroy)
# carry the same shape; only the URL vocabulary differs, because this is the
# one Omeka *S* site in the set. Omeka S namespaces every public URL under
# /s/<site-slug>/, and there is exactly one site here: Material-Histories.

@needs_html {
	not path *.*       # already has an extension: .html (no loop), assets
	not path */        # directories already resolve to index.html
	not path /files/*  # object storage; hashes must reach their own handler
	# Caddy runs every `redir` before any `respond`, so the 410 block at the
	# bottom of this file only ever fires for paths excluded here. Without
	# these lines /admin would 301 to /admin.html and 404.
	not path /api /api/* /api-context /admin /admin/* /login /logout /install
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The site root. The crawl produced two byte-identical copies of the home page
# -- one at / and one at /s/Material-Histories.html -- because Omeka S serves
# it at three URLs. The archive keeps one, at /, and these send the other two
# there. Without this the generic rule below would 301 /s/Material-Histories
# to a file that no longer exists.
@site_root path /s/Material-Histories /s/Material-Histories/ /s/Material-Histories/page/home /s/Material-Histories/page/home/
redir @site_root / 301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search. Omeka S ran full-text search at /s/<site>/index/search with a
# `fulltext_search` parameter, and offered two advanced-search forms at
# /s/<site>/item/search and /s/<site>/item-set/search. All three were database
# queries. search.html reads the same `fulltext_search` parameter, so a link
# of the old shape still lands on a real result set; {query} carries it over
# verbatim, and an empty one simply opens the search page.
@omeka_search path /s/Material-Histories/index/search /s/Material-Histories/index/search/
redir @omeka_search /search.html?{query} 302

@omeka_advanced_search path /s/Material-Histories/item/search /s/Material-Histories/item-set/search
redir @omeka_advanced_search /search.html?{query} 302

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browse. Omeka S paginated and sorted these server-side
# (?sort_by=created&sort_order=desc&page=2); the archive lists all 30 items on
# one page, so {path} deliberately drops the query rather than 301-ing to a
# page number that no longer exists. The trailing-slash forms are spelled out
# because `not path */` makes @needs_html skip them, and they would otherwise
# find no index.html and 404 on their own.
@item_browse path /s/Material-Histories/item /s/Material-Histories/item/
redir @item_browse /s/Material-Histories/item.html 301

# The trailing-slash form only. Without the slash this path is caught by the
# generic rule, which already produces exactly the right target.
redir /s/Material-Histories/item-set/ /s/Material-Histories/item-set.html 301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One page the origin itself 404s on. Two links inside the archived content
# promised a video-recording page at this slug; it was never published, and
# the recording it meant is on the "continuing the conversation" page. The
# in-page links are repaired to point there, and this covers anything outside
# the archive that followed the same dead link.
redir /s/Material-Histories/page/four-objects-video /s/Material-Histories/page/four-objects-blog.html 301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Endpoints that no longer exist in any form. The REST API served the JSON
# that this retrofit was built from and is the one thing a static host cannot
# stand in for; admin and login belong to an application that is gone. 410
# says so, rather than letting the generic rule turn them into a 404 that
# looks like a mistake.
@gone path /api /api/* /api-context /admin /admin/* /login /logout /install
respond @gone 410

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The generic rule. {path} drops the query, which is correct here: every
# query-carrying URL the origin served is a sort, a page number or a search,
# and each is handled above.
redir @needs_html {path}.html 301
